{"step_index":351,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T10:09:42Z","content":"The reason `ip route add` returned **`Error: Nexthop has invalid gateway`** is because of your subnet masks:\n\n* **Backend (`172.30.198.49`)** is in subnet `172.30.198.48/29` (gateway: `172.30.198.54`).\n* **Reverse Proxy (`172.30.198.33`)** is in subnet `172.30.198.32/29`.\n\nBecause they are in two different `/29` subnets, Linux cannot use `.33` as a direct Layer-2 nexthop gateway.\n\n---\n\n### Crucial Clarification Regarding Your Note:\n> *\"It should be noted that 109.62.64.18:9443 redirects to Map [172.30.198.50].\"*\n\nWhere is this redirection defined?\n\n1. **Case A: Is the Firewall DNAT rule itself pointing directly to `172.30.198.50:9443` (bypassing the reverse proxy)?**\n   * If the firewall was configured to send `109.62.64.18:9443` directly to Map (`172.30.198.50`), then the reverse proxy is completely bypassed by the firewall!\n2. **Case B: Is the Firewall DNAT pointing to `172.30.198.33:9443` (Reverse Proxy), and the Reverse Proxy performs the redirection to Map (`172.30.198.50:9443`)?**\n   * This is what we configured earlier on Nginx, and it is working.\n\n---\n\n### How Centreon Map Actually Works Between the Backend and the User\n\nIn Centreon Web (`172.30.198.49`), in **Administration $\\rightarrow$ Extensions $\\rightarrow$ Map**:\nWhat URL is configured for the Centreon Map Server?\n\n* **If it uses the hostname `https://map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443/`**:\n  We can simply add **one entry** in `/etc/hosts` on `th2-centprop-be1` (`172.30.198.49`):\n  ```bash\n  echo \"172.30.198.33 map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\" >> /etc/hosts\n  ```\n  * **When the backend connects**: it resolves `map.extranetclient...` to `172.30.198.33` (which routes fine via its gateway `172.30.198.54` without any public IP / hairpin issues).\n  * **When the user connects from outside**: the user resolves `map.extranetclient...` to `109.62.64.18` and goes through the firewall.\n\n* **If it is literally hardcoded with the raw IP `https://109.62.64.18:9443/`**:\n  The only place that can route internal traffic destined for `109.62.64.18` is the **Firewall (`172.30.198.54`)**, because both `.49` and `.33` have `.54` as their only default gateway. The firewall must have a **Hairpin NAT policy** enabled for subnet `172.30.198.48/29`.\n\nCan you check whether Centreon Web has the hostname (`map.extranetclient...`) or the raw IP in its Map configuration?","thinking":"**Diagnosing network route failure**\n\nThe route addition failed due to an invalid gateway. Observing that the target IP and the specified gateway are on different subnets explains the error. A Linux system requires a gateway to be within the local subnet to route traffic, which is not the case here.\n\n\n**Investigating Centreon Map Configuration**\n\nA potential VLAN mismatch exists if the gateway isn't reachable via ARP without proxy ARP, causing the route addition to fail. The user's note about the redirect suggests a reverse proxy setup, which is being diagrammed to understand the communication flow. The backend server might be misconfigured.\nThe Centreon Web configuration for the Map server URL is the focus, specifically if it's pointing to the external or internal address.\n\n\n"}
